=== REMOVED ===
# pivot.go was authored typed-clean in phase-39 (commit f7f7ecebe) — it
# never contained string-parse fallbacks (strconv.ParseFloat, parseEnum)
# nor compound-flatten logic for DoorState / Location / TireLocation /
# Time. Those responsibilities have always lived in:
#   - state_reader_log.go::unpackLocationCompounds (historical Location
#     JSONB blob expansion at the SQL boundary — NOT in pivot's allowed-
#     files scope)
#   - redis_cache.go (cache-side string coercion — NOT in pivot's
#     allowed-files scope)
# Therefore prompt 0063's "delete X / Y / Z" steps are no-ops at the
# code level. Doc-only updates were applied to make the post-phase-42
# typed-primitive contract explicit:
#   - pivot.go package doc: added a paragraph stating values are typed
#     primitives (float64/float32, int32/int64, bool, string, time.Time)
#     emitted by the codec, that pivot does NO string parsing and NO
#     compound flattening, and that historical Location compound
#     expansion is the responsibility of state_reader_log.go (not this
#     file). Cross-references ADR-004 §2.
#   - rawEvent.Signal doc: clarified that codec emits already-flattened
#     atomic names (LocationLatitude, DoorStateDriverFront,
#     TpmsHardWarningsFrontLeft) — pivot does not split or rejoin.
#   - rawEvent.Value doc: enumerated typed primitive kinds (float64,
#     float32, int32, int64, bool, string, time.Time, plus JSON-decoded
#     slices/maps for the few wire-structured signals) and reaffirmed
#     forwardFold treats Value as opaque (any).
#
# pivot_test.go: existing tests already used typed inputs end-to-end
# (float64 65.0 / 80.0, string "D" / "x" / "y", nil). No parse-error
# tests existed (none asserted strconv.ParseFloat error paths) so there
# was nothing to delete. ONE additive test was appended,
# TestForwardFold_PreservesTypedPrimitives_Phase42, which flows a
# heterogeneous mix of float64, float32, int32, int64, bool, string,
# and time.Time atomics through forwardFold and asserts identity is
# preserved per primitive kind via type-asserted equality. This locks
# the type-agnostic contract into the test surface so a future change
# that re-introduces value coercion (e.g. forcing everything to float64)
# would break the test.

